<title>USA: Mongolia MFN studied by U.S. House panel.</title>
<headline>Mongolia MFN studied by U.S. House panel.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>House Ways and Means Trade Subcommittee chairman Phil Crane said Monday he has asked for public comment on the extension of unconditional Most Favored Nation trade status to Mongolia.</p>
<p>Mongolia's trade status is currently subject to annual waivers of U.S. trade laws which govern MFN trade status to non-market economy countries.</p>
<p>After MFN was granted in 1991, two-way trade between the United States and Mongolia rose from $13 million in 1991 to $35 million in 1996.</p>
</text>
